ts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e41 [third_party_view; Justin Pope, The Motley Fool; original source Wall Street analysts (consensus) ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] The Motley Fool reported that Wall Street analysts estimate Nvidia's revenue could roughly double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year and $554 billion the following year.\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\n- e85 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source Wall Street analysts (consensus, unnamed) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for Nvidia in 2026 (\"this year\") and 41% revenue growth next year, as cited by the fool.com article without naming the individual analysts.\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e124 [rumour; Charlotte Trueman, datacenterdynamics.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release to 2028 from an originally slated 2027, with the research firm attributing the setback to manufacturability issues in the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules. Kyber is designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via Nvidia's NVLink 7 Switch.\n    Quote: Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported. … The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027. … However, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the p\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e125 [rumour; Charlotte Trueman, datacenterdynamics.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back to achieve similar power output, has been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its 'odd design and heavy operational burden', leaving Nvidia with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra.'\n    Quote: “Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” SemiAnalysis said, adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy op\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e94; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e126 [rumour; Charlotte Trueman, datacenterdynamics.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis noted that the manufacturing issues will also affect Nvidia's planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning the system will likely be delayed or limited to small volumes.\n    Quote: The manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e180, e190; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e127 [rumour; Charlotte Trueman, datacenterdynamics.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis separately reported, about a week before this item, that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of a two-chiplet design, also citing 'manufacturing execution concerns', with the two-die offering to deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n    Quote: The news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” … The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e171, e175, e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e130 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Wall Street analysts (consensus) ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA's revenue to grow 82% over the rest of calendar 2026 and 41% in calendar 2027, according to Keithen Drury in fool.com.\n    Quote: For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e176 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson (reported by Investing.com via uk.finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-06; from gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b 2026-07-06] An Nvidia spokesperson refuted SemiAnalysis's delay claims in a statement emailed to Investing.com, saying \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e146 (); both are in force: The article reports that NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-05; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's projection that 2027 spending could reach $1 trillion, which sits at rung 5. The implied outcome is about $1 trillion: the projection is a 2027 figure, compared with about $650 billion in 2026, and therefore points to the rung-5 boundary. Although phrased as 'could reach,' \n- v15 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) By how much will worldwide semiconductor industry revenue grow in calendar 2026 over 2025?: now rung 2 10% to below 20%; judged 2026-07-05; reasons: The supplied materials contain no market consensus, industry guidance, or stated worldwide 2026 growth plan, so there is no reference rung to locate. The South Korean investment headline is directionally supportive but gives no timeframe or revenue impact; Nvidia's bullish FY2027 conclusion is not a\n- v4 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-05; reasons: The reference is the four hyperscalers’ stated plan of around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3. The reported delays or cancellations of at least 75 projects push the forecast below that plan, but the article gives neither project-level spending nor evidence that those projects belong to t\n- v5 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2028-01-31) How will server DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 compare with calendar 2026?: now rung 3 Flat; judged 2026-07-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is no change from 2026, which sits at rung 3; no market consensus, company guidance, or price target is supplied. The new evidence strengthens the case for continuing upward pressure in 2027, but it does not quantify server DRAM or HBM contract prices or establish that the 2027 average\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-05; reasons: The reference is not independently locatable: no market consensus, Chinese authorization plan, or stated deadline expectation is supplied. The most likely outcome remains no public Chinese authorization by January 31, 2027. China was still described as having locked Nvidia out as of July 2, 2026; U.\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The market reference is Wall Street analysts' roughly $554 billion estimate for the following fiscal year, which sits in rung 2. The 41% growth estimate applied to $392 billion implies about $553 billion, also rung 2. The revenue-sharing program is a possible upside source but has no disclosed scale, and the updated AI-capex and semiconductor themes do not quantify Nvidia's share of spending or support a specific uplift across a rung boundary.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views."
